# Workspaces and layout

Paneform keeps a model of connected displays, workspaces, and windows. Commands act on this model and the layout engine applies the resulting positions.

## Workspaces

A workspace groups windows. The focused workspace is the one you are viewing. Use `wm workspace focus <workspace>` to change it; a new name can be created on first use.

## Tiling and floating

In BSP mode, tiled windows share the available area. A floating window still
belongs to a workspace — it keeps its assigned workspace and appears in that
workspace's window list — but it does not participate in that workspace's
tiling layout. The manager does not reposition or resize it: it keeps the frame
it had, and the remaining tiled windows are laid out as if it were not there.
Floating windows can overlap tiled windows; the focused window is frontmost.

`wm window float --toggle` switches the focused window (or `--window <id>`)
between the two states. A floating window that is toggled joins the tiling and
its peers resize to make room; a tiled window that is toggled keeps its tile's
frame and floats above the re-tiled workspace. See the
[command reference](/wm/docs/commands/) for the exact forms.

## Pausing

Pause holds automatic mutations. Resume to let the manager reconcile the desktop again.
